% ============================================================================
% energy-sources — the common sources we get energy from and whether each one
% is RENEWABLE or NONRENEWABLE (adj-facts-stdlib, PHYSICS).
% ============================================================================
% A first big idea of energy literacy, and a rung an ENERGY / EARTH-SCIENCE
% agent reasons UP from: the things we get energy from split into two families.
% A RENEWABLE source is "constantly replenished by nature" — the sun keeps
% shining, the wind keeps blowing, the rivers keep flowing — so it is always
% there when we need it. A NONRENEWABLE source is one whose "supplies are
% limited to what we can mine or extract from the earth, and they take a very
% long time to form" — burn the coal and it is gone. Before you can reason
% about a country's energy mix, a power plant, or a climate policy you must
% first know WHICH family a given source belongs to. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? energy_source_class(solar, $Class)     % renewable
%
% This is DISTINCT from the sibling `physics/energy-forms` library, which maps a
% FORM of energy (chemical, thermal, nuclear, …) to the word that defines it.
% This table is about SOURCES — where the energy comes from — and their
% renewability, not about the physical form the energy takes.
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `energy_source_class(energy_source, class)` carrying the table's
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the class WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on a word that is not one of these enumerated sources (it never
% invents a classification).
%
% Each source the U.S. EIA "Sources of energy" page enumerates, and the family
% it places that source in, as a little truth table:
%
%     energy source   class          the source's own list it comes from
%     -------------   ------------   ------------------------------------------
%     solar           renewable      "the five major renewable energy sources"
%     wind            renewable      "the five major renewable energy sources"
%     hydropower      renewable      "the five major renewable energy sources"
%     geothermal      renewable      "the five major renewable energy sources"
%     biomass         renewable      "the five major renewable energy sources"
%     coal            nonrenewable   nonrenewable energy "includes: … Coal …"
%     natural_gas     nonrenewable   nonrenewable energy "includes: … Natural gas …"
%     petroleum       nonrenewable   nonrenewable energy "includes: Petroleum …"
%     nuclear         nonrenewable   nonrenewable energy "includes: … Nuclear energy"
%
% The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a class and recall every source in it:
%
%     ? energy_source_class($Source, nonrenewable)   % coal / natural_gas / …
%
% A word that is NOT one of these enumerated sources — `magnetic`, `lightning`,
% `rock` — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than
% inventing a class. That honest-abstention property is what makes the table
% safe to build a reasoner on: it answers exactly the source→class pairs it can
% ground, and only those.
%
% The `class` value of every row is a SINGLE lowercase atom, `renewable` or
% `nonrenewable` — the EIA page's own two families — chosen only where the page
% EXPLICITLY names the source inside one of the two lists, never inferred.
% (`natural_gas` uses an underscore so the two-word source name stays one atom;
% the source lists it as "Natural gas".)
%
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
% Provenance (nothing here is asserted from memory — every source→class pair
% below was read out of a single fetched U.S. EIA "Energy Explained" page, and
% any pair the page did not place in one of its two lists would have been
% dropped). The two spans that fix every row:
%
%   renewable (solar, wind, hydropower, geothermal, biomass)
%     https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php
%     (U.S. Energy Information Administration — Energy Explained)
%     "The five major renewable energy sources include: Solar energy from the
%      sun, Geothermal energy from heat inside the earth, Wind energy from
%      uneven heating from the sun, Biomass from plants, Hydropower from flowing
%      water" — and: "Renewable energy sources are constantly replenished by
%      nature, which means they will always be there when we need them."
%
%   nonrenewable (coal, natural_gas, petroleum, nuclear)
%     https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php
%     (U.S. Energy Information Administration — Energy Explained)
%     "In the United States, nonrenewable energy is the primary source of the
%      energy we use, which includes: Petroleum, Hydrocarbon gas liquids,
%      Natural gas, Coal, Nuclear energy" — and: "These energy sources are
%      called nonrenewable because their supplies are limited to what we can
%      mine or extract from the earth, and they take a very long time to form."
%
% (The EIA list also names "Hydrocarbon gas liquids" as nonrenewable; it is NOT
% shipped as a row because it is not one of the everyday energy sources this
% table enumerates — an intentional drop, not an oversight.)
%
% An ADJ `table` carries ONE provenance envelope, so the `source`/`locator`/
% `trust` below hold the single cleanest span: the EIA statement that fixes the
% first row (solar → renewable). Every OTHER row is grounded by one of the two
% spans above, so each class remains independently checkable. The source is the
% U.S. Energy Information Administration, a primary U.S. government teaching
% resource, so `trust authoritative`.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table energy_source_class {
    columns energy_source, class

    row (solar, renewable)
    row (wind, renewable)
    row (hydropower, renewable)
    row (geothermal, renewable)
    row (biomass, renewable)
    row (coal, nonrenewable)
    row (natural_gas, nonrenewable)
    row (petroleum, nonrenewable)
    row (nuclear, nonrenewable)

    source "The five major renewable energy sources include: Solar energy from the sun, Geothermal energy from heat inside the earth, Wind energy from uneven heating from the sun, Biomass from plants, Hydropower from flowing water"
    locator "https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php"
    trust authoritative
}
